  "duration": 104.44000244140625,
  "text": "The employees called them gaming strategies. Management called them sales practices. The lawyers would eventually call them fraud. Here's how it worked. A customer would come in to open a checking account, simple enough. But the banker would also open a savings account and a credit card, and online banking with a new password, all without the customer's knowledge. The signatures were forged. The email addresses were fake, often just the customer's real email with extra numbers added. The pins for debit cards were set to 000. Some employees got creative. They would open accounts using the identities of elderly customers who wouldn't notice. They would sign up immigrants who couldn't read the English paperwork. They would create accounts for people who had died. The board's investigation found a branch manager's teenage daughter had 24 accounts, an adult daughter had 18, and the manager's spouse had 21. The pressure was relentless. Employees received multiple emails per day tracking their progress toward quotas. Managers held four o'clock huddles, where they would berate underperformers in front of their colleagues. Those who missed their numbers were put on performance improvement plans. The first step toward termination. One former employee described it as a choice, cheat or starve. Another said she developed an ulcer. A third said he vomited before work every morning. And when they reported what was happening, the ethics hotline became a pipeline to unemployment.",
